WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.120
Vsauce!

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:08.010
Kevin here, with 64 kittens, 2 wallets, 1
coin -- and some math that lies to you.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:13.250
Some of you are gonna be okay with that, because
you’ll already have found cognitive shelter

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:15.030
in the unknown.

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:17.150
Others won’t even see it.

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:23.400
I’ve made a lot of videos about different
probability problems and paradoxes -- Russian

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:28.880
roulette, Monty Hall, the Birthday Paradox
-- and… we’ve solved them all.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:34.050
But what happens when the math is right and
there’s still a paradox?

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:39.829
What happens when you KNOW a right answer
is actually wrong?

00:00:39.829 --> 00:00:41.100
Wallets.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:45.739
Imagine I choose two people randomly as they
walk on the streets of Manhattan.

00:00:45.739 --> 00:00:49.129
I give them the option to play a game.

00:00:49.129 --> 00:00:53.719
They compare the amount of money in their
wallets, and the person with the LOWER amount

00:00:53.719 --> 00:00:57.670
wins all the money in the other wallet.

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:00.289
Does either person have an advantage?

00:01:00.289 --> 00:01:02.600
The math says NO.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:04.460
YOU say YES.

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:06.800
But wallets can wait.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:11.890
Let’s start with coin flips, the probability
of which is perfectly quantifiable.

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:16.860
I’ve got a coin with YES on one side and
NO on the other.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:22.560
The odds of getting a YES or NO are 50/50…
other than the 1 in 5,000 chance that it lands

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:23.580
on its edge.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.680
So 50/50.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:34.130
The odds of getting YES / NO / YES / NO / YES
are .5 raised to the power of 5, or .5 x .5

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:38.940
x .5 x .5 x .5 = .03125.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:39.940
Cool.

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:43.520
The odds of getting 5 YES’s or NO’s in
a row?

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:44.520
WELL!

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:45.520
Now.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:48.920
That’s… also .5 raised to the power of
5.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:54.750
Every combination of YES and NO has exactly
the same chance of occurring.

00:01:54.750 --> 00:02:01.240
But you KNOW deep down that getting 5 in a
row doesn’t happen nearly as often as a

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:03.140
mix of the two.

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:11.239
How can two things have the exact same probability
of occurring, yet occur less often?

00:02:11.239 --> 00:02:12.739
I can explain….

00:02:12.739 --> 00:02:13.739
WITH KITTENS.

00:02:13.739 --> 00:02:16.260
Real quick, this Vsauce video is sponsored
by Vsauce.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:20.620
The brand new Curiosity Box by Vsauce will
give your body a shirt.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:22.180
Your hands a puzzle.

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:24.219
Your eyeballs illusions.

00:02:24.219 --> 00:02:26.020
Your bacteria a kit.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:27.489
Your brain a book.

00:02:27.489 --> 00:02:32.230
And Curiosity Curriculum cards detailing the
science and activities behind it all.

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:36.159
So subscribe now to the Vsauce Curiosity Box.

00:02:36.159 --> 00:02:42.230
And let Vsauce into your home.

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:48.989
You have a litter of kittens (or your cat
does) -- let’s say the cat has 4 kittens,

00:02:48.989 --> 00:02:54.010
and there’s a 50/50 chance of each kitten
being a boy or a girl.

00:02:54.010 --> 00:03:02.349
Every combination of kittens is .5 raised
to the power of 4, or .0625 -- but the most

00:03:02.349 --> 00:03:08.469
common combination isn’t the equal split
of 2 boys / 2 girls you’d expect.

00:03:08.469 --> 00:03:13.049
It’s actually 3 of one and 1 of the other.

00:03:13.049 --> 00:03:18.480
When you map out all the possible combinations
of kittens, there are 16 combinations that

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:24.640
all have the same exact probability of happening
-- but getting a perfectly even 2 of each

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:29.879
happens in just 6 of the 16 combos, or 37.5%.

00:03:29.879 --> 00:03:36.170
A 3-1 split happens in 8 of the 16 combinations.

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:43.389
50% of the time you’ll get 3 and 1, despite
the 50/50 math telling you everything’s

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:45.849
got the same chance.

00:03:45.849 --> 00:03:52.209
Simple math tells you one thing, but combinatorics
using that math tells you another.

00:03:52.209 --> 00:03:53.209
Okay.

00:03:53.209 --> 00:03:55.760
Let’s go back to wallets.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:59.370
Do you have an advantage in this game?

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:04.930
If your wallet has less money in it, you’ll
always win MORE than that amount by taking

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:07.879
the other wallet that has more money in it.

00:04:07.879 --> 00:04:12.980
If YOUR wallet has more money, you’ll only
lose what you’ve got.

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:18.669
Since the game is totally random and neither
player knows anything about the other, the

00:04:18.669 --> 00:04:24.810
probability of each player winning and losing
is the same -- but when you win, you’ll

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:28.250
get more money than you LOSE when you DON’T
win.

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:32.810
So you’ve got a major advantage in this
game.

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:38.080
But when the other player loses, they’re
only losing what’s in THEIR wallet.

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:41.930
And when they win, they’re winning MORE
than what’s in their wallet.

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:46.580
THEY’VE got a major advantage in this game.

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:53.350
The question is: How can two players competing
against each other BOTH have an advantage?

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:54.430
Here’s how…

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:55.520
THEY CAN’T!

00:04:55.520 --> 00:05:00.530
If there are two outcomes -- X and Y -- we
can assign probabilities to each, and adding

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:05.229
those probabilities together HAS TO add up
to 1.

00:05:05.229 --> 00:05:11.590
Having an advantage would mean a probability
greater than .5… if X and Y are both greater

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:17.280
than .5, you’re gonna get more than 1 when
you add them -- and that’s probabilistically

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:19.150
impossible.

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:24.520
It would be like betting on a sports game
where Vegas says BOTH teams are favored to

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:25.520
win.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:27.849
It makes no sense.

00:05:27.849 --> 00:05:28.849
And look.

00:05:28.849 --> 00:05:32.690
Maurice Kraitchik’s payoff matrix for the
wallet game shows that the two players have

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:35.850
exactly the same probabilities of winning.

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:41.180
It really is 50/50, and the math proves it.

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:45.250
But the logic of each player is… flawless.

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:47.120
It’s unassailable.

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:52.879
Neither player CAN have an advantage, but
both players have perfectly reasonable logic

00:05:52.879 --> 00:05:56.949
suggesting each one DOES have an advantage.

00:05:56.949 --> 00:06:03.629
The problem here is that you NEED to know,
and discarding information makes it easier.

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:08.530
I’ll talk more about this in a future video
-- but think how much easier this problem

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:14.360
gets when you just trust the math, or you
go with logic and your gut.

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:21.259
Reducing a complex scenario between math and
reality lets you eliminate the paradox and

00:06:21.259 --> 00:06:23.229
proceed comfortably.

00:06:23.229 --> 00:06:29.810
But when faced with explaining the faulty
logic of these two wallets, some of the greatest

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:35.620
math minds could only proceed comfortably
knowing that they... couldn’t really tell

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:36.620
ya.

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:41.990
Kraitchik first proposed the problem in 1944
-- and The Two Envelopes Paradox was conceived

00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:45.620
as a derivative of it in 1953.

00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:51.810
Nearly 80 years later, we don’t have a clear
answer to why the math works and the reality

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:54.010
doesn’t.

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:59.639
You can factor in discrete distributions,
random variables, or continuous density functions

00:06:59.639 --> 00:07:07.710
to get deep into the math -- but the TRUTH
is that probability tells you one thing, and

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:10.479
logic tells you another.

00:07:10.479 --> 00:07:15.060
The moment we know something about the players
in the wallet game, we enter the territory

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:21.240
of the subjectivistic interpretation of probability
-- the Bayesian notion of utilizing information

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:27.340
about a problem to assign possible values
to different frequencies of outcome.

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:32.620
So like, if one player is 18 years old, you
might think they’re less likely to have

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:38.419
a lot of money in their wallet because younger
people are more likely to earn less, carry

00:07:38.419 --> 00:07:40.410
cards or pay with their phones.

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:47.210
If a player is older and on vacation in Manhattan,
their wallet might be more likely to be LOADED

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:48.210
with cash.

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:53.039
But in our wallet game, we don’t know ANYTHING.

00:07:53.039 --> 00:07:58.780
We have math that proves it’s equal and
logic that proves it isn’t.

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:05.349
We have such a strong tendency toward equiprobability
that when logic tells us we’ve got an exception

00:08:05.349 --> 00:08:11.930
to a normal 50/50 rule, it shocks us into
taking it too seriously.

00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:17.800
It messes with our uniformity belief enough
to make us trust it.

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:23.759
Martin Gardner wrote that Kraitchik was “no
help” about logic in the wallet paradox.

00:08:23.759 --> 00:08:26.460
Gardner couldn’t explain it, either.

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:32.909
All these papers by very smart mathematicians
get into the math but just discard the very

00:08:32.909 --> 00:08:35.930
real logical objection.

00:08:35.930 --> 00:08:42.590
Often by concluding with something along the
lines of, "uhhh... don't play this game!"

00:08:42.590 --> 00:08:47.750
No one can explain why without making a few
assumptions.

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:53.970
Maybe the wallet game is just an antinomical
paradox that we have to live with.

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:56.070
But the coin flips and kittens?

00:08:56.070 --> 00:09:00.440
That’s the type of math that can mislead
you.

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:05.870
Probability just isn’t frequency -- and
frequency is reality.

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:14.940
So life may be a series of 50/50’s, wins
and losses, yeses and nos -- that lead us

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:18.340
all to wildly different outcomes.

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:27.040
Like sitting at a whiteboard table with 64
kittens and a pink leopard print wallet.

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:28.279
And as always -- thanks for watching.

